finding: P2 scripts/check-example-ui.node.ts:223-239 — The value-level scan does not tokenize CSS escapes. The in-scope stylesheet `.a { color: r\65 d; }` independently returns [] through colors("a.css", source), although r\65 d is the CSS identifier red. parseCss preserves the escape in its textual value and the named-colour regex never decodes it. Conversely, a valid quoted content string with an escaped quote, `.a { content: "\"red"; color: #eee9df; }`, reports `color: red`: the string-removal regex ends at the escaped quote and treats string content as a colour. Thus literal CSS colour escapes can pass, while out-of-colour string content can fail. Handle CSS identifiers/strings with their escape rules rather than assuming the parsed declaration value is tokenized. Independently examined the parser-based extractor, declarations visitor and value scanner; ran all 20 check tests (76 assertions, passing). Additional probes confirm invalid CSS parse failures are reported, ordinary commented named colours are refused, and system colours Canvas/CanvasText are refused. Previous unquoted-attribute, markup-looking stylesheet and excluded-script issues are addressed by the new extraction. No edits or src/extras/fog examination.
